      "id": 1,
      "claim": "In a DFS of a connected undirected graph, every edge that is not a tree edge is a back edge connecting a vertex to one of its ancestors in the DFS tree.",
      "evidence": "When DFS explores an edge (x,y), if y is already visited then y must be on the current recursion stack (the path from the root to x); otherwise the edge would have been taken as a tree edge when y was first discovered. In an undirected graph no cross or forward edges can appear.",
      "weight": 0.4,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Given a non‑tree edge (u,v) with pre(u) < pre(v), u is an ancestor of v in the DFS tree.",
      "evidence": "From step 1 the edge is a back edge, so one endpoint is an ancestor of the other. Preorder numbers increase when a vertex is first discovered; the earlier discovered endpoint (smaller preorder) must be the ancestor.",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "If u is an ancestor of v, then the lowest common ancestor of u and v is u.",
      "evidence": "Definition of lowest common ancestor: the deepest vertex that is ancestor of both. When one vertex is ancestor of the other, that ancestor is the deepest common ancestor.",
      "weight": 0.1,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "For an ancestor u and descendant v, post(v) < post(u); therefore statement I (post(u) < post(v)) is false.",
      "evidence": "In a postorder traversal a vertex is recorded after all vertices in its subtree have been recorded. Hence every descendant finishes before its ancestor.",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": [2]
    }
  ],
  "answer": "D",
